# ترجمة 17–21
T = {
"16": {
"en": """The Fish Alley, as I like to call it. Some of the residents of the Nakkasah market (Qawz al-Nakkasah), from the Rohingya (Burmese) community, call it that too. The alley is sixty meters long and a meter wide, or a little more. On both banks of the alley, spice shops crowd against fish stalls, and some of the meat-cutting tables stretch out to the waist of the road and narrow it further still. Yet the flow of people through the alley never stops for a moment. I said the alley is sixty meters long, but in meaning it is sixty years.

You enter the alley from the south, where the market square roars with the babble of people: a seller raising his voice to hawk his unsold goods; a buyer haggling over the price of a kilo of green beans as though bargaining with a deaf man; and hundreds of hens waiting to be executed on the charge of hoarding protein. So you leave the square at your back and slip in, beside Abdulaziz’s grocery, to the sleeping alley that runs north. As you set your foot at its head, you realize that you are cut off, in some mysterious way, from the heap of noise behind you, as though a gate of magic stood at the entrance and wrapped the senses of newcomers in a gentle numbness. As you take your first steps you feel a platoon of ants crawling up your back and neck. These are the sellers’ eyes, inspecting you, stranger. Whatever the case, I find a certain intoxication in those looks. Those eyes see me as something new, after I have worn thin in the eyes of the people with whom I share the same cultural mainstream. And I carry a camera, and in the lexicon of street vendors a camera is an omen of bad luck. I will not forget the time I entered the same market one afternoon, and my presence with a camera happened to coincide with a municipal raid confiscating the goods of violators. Naturally, many people took me for an informer, and one of the sellers told me frankly that the devil in my hand was to blame. I don’t know how he tied the two events together.

I am certain that the nervous system with which you cross this sixty-meter alley is not the one you started out with. The density of shapes, faces, sounds, smells, and colors within a single frame of the eye makes a surreal picture that is hard to repeat, for every element is in ceaseless motion and eternal undulation. You will hear the shuffle of passersby’s sandals in the mud, breathe in a piece of the battle between the scent of pepper and the smell of fish in the air, touch the legs of a cow, and taste the sourness of the air, all in the same moment. A rich experience, which I will write about in coming pictures, with notes on the situation of my Rohingya brothers there, some light on their history, and an interrogation of their faces.

Mr. Jamal Kutbi accompanied me, thanks to him. The picture is from the alley, under the shop lights, with no outside additions.""",
"notes": ["Qawz means a dune or rise of ground. Nakkasah is a market quarter in Makkah."],
},

"17": {
"en": """Doves of peace: children from the community we call, in Makkah, the “Barmawi” (Burmese). Most of those who emigrated from Burma during the ethnic and religious persecution belong to the Muslim Rohingya nation, which had settled in Arakan, the coastal region of Burma. Arakan was originally an independent state, ruled in succession by Muslim families between 1430 and 1784 CE, before the Burmese king “Bodawpaya” occupied it and made it wholly Buddhist. Since then the ethnic and sectarian conflict between Buddhists and Muslims has raged, leaving bloody battles and leading the authorities to take an exclusionary stance toward the Rohingya, which began with a bitter persecution that has never ended. Part of it was stripping them of the rights of citizenship in 1982, after several constitutional amendments, taking their nationality, so that the homeland was finally taken from them, after the very name of their land (Arakan) had been taken from them before. Since then the Rohingya knows his land only in the memory of stories and in dark daydreams.

The Rohingya emigrated out of Burma in waves (to Bangladesh, India, and Saudi Arabia…), the first in 1942 after a massacre whose horror words fall short of describing and before which minds fail. Had it not been for research and inquiry, I would have known nothing of their story, I who lived beside them, studied among them, and memorized the Qur’an at the hands of their worthies. In any case, the history of Muslim settlement in Arakan goes back to the seventh century CE, when Arab and Yemeni merchants used to visit “Akyab,” the capital of Arakan, by sea. That is why many researchers hold that the Rohingya are descendants of the Arabs of the Arabian Peninsula, of the Moorish Arabs of Andalusia, of Afghans and Persians. The language of the country was Persian until 1845, that is, sixty-one years after it fell to King “Bodawpaya”!

I remember that Nur, the auto-body man who owns the workshop I use, told me once in his Burmese accent, after we had wandered in conversation over everything, as he tried to straighten a dent in my car door: “Our origins are Arab, from Yemen!” He had hardly finished the sentence before a wicked laugh tried to escape from my belly, which God’s kindness and a remnant of good manners held back. My laughter then came from the sheer irony between his accent and the origin he ascribed to himself. I neither believed him nor disbelieved him, though, God forgive me, I reduced his claim at the time to a feeling of inequality: an attempt on his part to cling to an identity that would let him feel equal (and of what equality are we even speaking!). I had no right to think that of him, no right to count color, race, or religion among the things that undermine equality in human rights. Here I am, Nur, four years later. I believe you, I write about you, and I am proud of you!""",
"notes": ["The Arabic says samkari, in Hijazi usage the man who repairs car bodywork.", "The historical claims about Arakan are the author’s own account, given here as he wrote them."],
},

"18": {
"en": """The poet Byron once said: “Man is a swing between laughter and tears.” But your face, my little one, is a swing between sorrow and beauty, as though sorrow and beauty in your face were twins who came out of one womb and were poured from one mold. The cloud of sorrow that wraps your face, little one, is not like the sorrow that usually touches children, when you see a child weeping with tear-wet eyes and a laughing heart: if he is given, he is content; if he is not given, he forgets. Why do I feel that behind your bright, lovely face lies an endless rainforest, and beneath your skin an old man schooled in the fields of experience, who plunged into the depths of the seas, and who, after the struggle, settled for stillness and found his way into your gentle skin to hide in it from the nose of death?

My lovely little one, your picture reminds me of myself as a child. I was always head-bowed, always lost in thought. I was not woven on anyone else’s loom, and I was not threaded onto the awl of a lavish childhood. I was a mute pearl, and sorrow pierced me. Your consolation, little one, is that sorrow clings most to lofty, translucent souls. The delicacy of your feeling is a breach through which sorrow attacks you. Yet I do not know in what way to turn my words to you, whether as consolation or as supplication. So take from them, little one, and leave the rest. What stopped me in you was only what I found in your child’s face: a marvelous model, a rare image of deep sorrow, the kind that strikes far into the soul. I could not have been stopped by sorrow or beauty, one or both, and not stopped, or been questioned by it and not spoken. “For the silent speaks in what it signifies, and the mute is eloquent in what it proves.” I write about you, and I keep your picture on the wall of my study. Be well, little one.""",
"notes": ["The Byron line is quoted as the author gives it."],
},

"19": {
"en": """It is as though al-Sayyab had been standing at the very spot from which I took this picture when he wrote the opening of his poignant poem “In the Old Market.” Al-Sayyab insisted on making the market at dusk the stage of his poem, as I wanted to do with my picture, because sunset in itself holds a deceptive, romantic meaning. On one hand it points to wilting, vanishing, parting: the sun sends its thin red threads over the place in a scene of farewell that stirs the hidden depths of the poetic soul and draws over the place an air of sadness, gloom, and unease. Even Nietzsche once said, through Zarathustra, as he watched the sun: “The twilight has drawn near, the sun has inclined toward its setting, and my happiness has hidden from me.” Arabic literature in particular, and human literature in general, overflow with evidence of the drama of sunset, as though people had agreed on this meaning of it. I once heard one of my sheikhs say: “The knowers are disturbed by the sight of sunset,” and they have their states in this matter.

On the other hand, sunset carries a meaning of passing beyond and moving on, if it is seen within the whole of the day. It is the key to the quiet night life, to dwelling and to rest. But this meaning soon turns on its owners, and is snatched up by drama once more. For what is the night but the food of lovers, the refuge of the grieving, the echo of the groans of the afflicted, and the ladder of the worshippers’ whispered prayers? And what are stillness and cold but stimulants to wounds, and questioners of painful memory? Perhaps man creates the meaning of his life through language, or perhaps language creates man and his life. I do not know. I leave you with the first piece of al-Sayyab’s poem:

The night, and the old market,
its voices hushed but for the murmuring of passersby
and the footsteps of the stranger… and what the wind pours out of sad melody
in that pitch-dark night.
The night, and the old market, and the murmuring of passersby,
and the light squeezed out by the sorrowful lamps, in pallor,
like fog on the road,
from every ancient shop,
among the pale faces, as though it were a melody dissolving
in that old market.""",
"notes": ["Badr Shakir al-Sayyab (1926–1964) was the Iraqi poet and a pioneer of modern free verse. The line from Zarathustra is quoted as the author gives it."],
},

"20": {
"en": """A Burmese elder selling fish in the Nakkasah market. He followed me with his eyes as I paced the alley back and forth, and when I noticed him watching me, with a child’s curiosity in his eyes, I asked his permission for a photograph. He did nothing more than nod yes with his eyes, unlike many who were wary of being photographed at first.

On the margin of the picture: the sight of old men who sell fish reminds me of what is reported about Abu Uthman al-Jahiz, the learned man of letters, the prince of eloquence. It is said of him that he sold fish by day so that he could rent, at night, the booksellers’ shops, to read in them. Who, then, among those who bought fish from al-Jahiz, knew that he was the writer whose books traveled to the horizons? In our heritage there are eminent scholars who were named for their trades, or for their fathers’ trades: al-Qaffal al-Shashi, the Shafi‘i master of his age, who worked at making locks; al-Zajjaj Abu Ishaq, who turned glass; Ibn al-Sarraj the grammarian, whose father was a saddler; Ibn al-Akfani, whose father sold shrouds; Ibn al-Sa‘ati al-Hanafi, whose father made clocks; and Ibn al-Sammak the great preacher, the son of a man who sold fish.

All of these are trades that most of us today consider lowly, and even in the old days the Bedouin did not recognize trades and looked down on their practitioners. It is enough that they derived mihna, “trade,” from the root m-h-n, a sound root that signifies the contempt of a thing, as the dictionary has it. True, the sense of degradation has left the word, because the conditions that gave rise to it have vanished. There are no longer conquering Bedouin set against farmers and artisans. But the reality of trades that depend on the skill and craft of the seller or maker, and then depend for their trade on people’s wishes and interests, does not raise them to the rank of work that yields great profit. Then government jobs arrived to keep the trades in their lowly place on the ladder, so they neither escaped the Bedouin’s domination and contempt, nor rose above government jobs.

Trades are noble in themselves, and I do not know by what door a critic of them walks in to lower their rank, or on what evidence he rests in belittling them. There is no better proof of their nobility than that prophets, leaders, and reformers of the world have graduated from the school of trades and crafts. So shall we ever hear of an Ibn al-Sammak again?""",
"notes": ["Al-Jahiz (c. 776–868) was the great prose writer of the Abbasid age. The scholars named here are medieval figures whose names or surnames come from their own or their fathers’ trades."],
},
}
